@c ============================================================
@node Mail Flow
@chapter Mail Flow

@section Inbound SMTP (Port 25)

@enumerate
@item
A remote MTA connects to port 25.  @command{tcpserver} accepts the
connection and spawns @command{qmail-smtpd} running as
@code{uid=vpopmail gid=vchkpw}.

@item
@command{qmail-smtpd} applies a 5-second greeting delay and drops
connections that send data before the greeting (pre-greet drop).

@item
The @code{EHLO} command triggers HELO-DNS checking (forward-confirmed
reverse DNS).  @code{RCPT TO} is checked against DNSBL blacklists.

@item
SPF is evaluated based on the sending IP and @code{MAIL FROM} domain.
Behaviour is configurable via @file{/etc/qmail/spfbehavior} (default:
log only).

@item
The chkuser patch verifies each @code{RCPT TO} recipient against the
vpopmail PostgreSQL database @emph{before} accepting the message.
Unknown recipients are rejected at SMTP time with a @code{553} response,
eliminating backscatter.

@item
After @code{DATA}, qmail atomically writes the message to
@file{/var/lib/qmail/queue/} via @command{qmail-queue}.

@item
@command{qmail-send} wakes, reads the queued message, and consults
@file{/etc/qmail/virtualdomains}.  For a message to
@email{user@@example.com} where @code{example.com:example.com} is in
@file{virtualdomains}, qmail rewrites the recipient to the local address
@code{example.com-user}.

@item
@command{qmail-lspawn} looks up @code{example.com-user} in
@file{/var/lib/qmail/users/cdb}.  The wildcard entry
@code{+example.com-:example.com:89:89:@dots{}} matches and routes
delivery to @command{qmail-local} running as @code{uid=vpopmail}.

@item
@command{qmail-local} reads
@file{/home/vpopmail/domains/example.com/.qmail-default}, which
contains:

@example
| /home/vpopmail/bin/vdelivermail '' delete
@end example

@item
@command{vdelivermail} looks up @code{user} in the vpopmail database,
confirms the Maildir path (@file{/home/vpopmail/domains/example.com/user/Maildir/}),
and writes the message to @file{Maildir/new/}.
@end enumerate

@section Outbound Submission (Port 587)

@enumerate
@item
An email client connects to port 587 through an SSH tunnel or directly.
@command{tcpserver} spawns @command{qmail-smtpd} with
@env{SMTPAUTH=!} (authentication required).

@item
The server issues @code{EHLO} advertising @code{STARTTLS}.  The client
initiates TLS using the certificate at @file{/etc/qmail/servercert.pem}
(owned @code{root:vchkpw 640}).

@item
After the TLS handshake, @command{qmail-smtpd} re-advertises
capabilities including @code{AUTH PLAIN LOGIN}.

@item
The client sends @code{AUTH PLAIN} or @code{AUTH LOGIN} with
@code{user@@domain} and password.  @command{qmail-smtpd} forks
@command{vchkpw}, which:

@enumerate a
@item
Reads @file{/home/vpopmail/etc/vpopmail.pgsql} for PostgreSQL
connection credentials.

@item
Queries @code{SELECT pw_passwd FROM vpopmail WHERE pw_name='user'
AND pw_domain='domain'}.

@item
Verifies the supplied password against the stored SHA-512 crypt hash.

@item
On success, execs the next program (@command{/bin/true}), setting
@env{RELAYCLIENT=""} which permits relaying.
@end enumerate

@item
The client sends @code{MAIL FROM}, @code{RCPT TO} (any external
address is now permitted due to @env{RELAYCLIENT}), and @code{DATA}.

@item
The message is queued via @command{qmail-queue}.

@item
@command{qmail-send} dispatches the message to @command{qmail-remote}.
If @file{/etc/qmail/filterargs} exists, @command{spawn-filter} is
invoked first to add a DKIM signature using the private key from
@file{/etc/qmail/domainkeys/<domain>/}.

@item
@command{qmail-remote} resolves the destination domain's MX records,
connects to the remote SMTP server, negotiates TLS if offered, and
delivers the message.  SRS rewriting is applied if the message is a
forward.
@end enumerate

@section System User Mail Delivery

Unix system users (e.g., @code{fade}) receive mail at
@email{fade@@hostname} via @command{qmail-local}, which reads
@file{~fade/.qmail}.  A typical @file{.qmail} for Maildir delivery:

@example
./Maildir/
@end example

To receive at @email{fade@@example.com} (a virtual domain), add an
exact-match entry to @file{/var/lib/qmail/users/assign} before any
wildcard for that domain:

@example
=example.com-fade:fade:1000:1000:/home/fade:::
@end example

Then rebuild the CDB and restart qmail-send:

@example
sudo /var/lib/qmail/bin/qmail-newu
sudo svc -t /etc/service/qmail-send
@end example


@c ============================================================
@node Authentication
@chapter Authentication Architecture

@section SMTP Authentication (Port 587)

@command{vchkpw} (@file{/home/vpopmail/bin/vchkpw}) is a
@emph{checkpassword}-compatible program.  It is setuid root, owned
@code{root:vchkpw}.  The @command{qmail-smtpd} process on port 587 runs
as @code{uid=vpopmail gid=vchkpw} and can therefore execute vchkpw.

Authentication flow:

@example
qmail-smtpd (port 587)
    |
    | fork + exec
    v
vchkpw
    |
    | read /home/vpopmail/etc/vpopmail.pgsql
    | connect to PostgreSQL (host|port|user|pass|db)
    | SELECT pw_passwd FROM vpopmail WHERE ...
    | verify SHA-512 crypt hash
    |
    +-- success: exec /bin/true; RELAYCLIENT="" set
    |
    +-- failure: exit 1; SMTP AUTH fails
@end example

The credentials file @file{/home/vpopmail/etc/vpopmail.pgsql} (owned
@code{root:vchkpw 640}) uses pipe-separated fields:

@example
host|port|user|password|database
localhost|5432|vpopmail|<password>|vpopmail
@end example

@section IMAP Authentication — Virtual Users

Dovecot authenticates virtual users (@email{user@@domain}) via the SQL
passdb driver, which queries the PostgreSQL vpopmail table using
credentials from @file{/etc/dovecot/dovecot-vpopmail-sql.conf.ext}
(generated by the @command{dovecot-vpopmail} postinst from
@file{vpopmail.pgsql}; owned @code{root:dovecot 640}).

The connection block format (Dovecot 2.4):

@example
sql_driver = pgsql
pgsql localhost @{
  parameters @{
    dbname = vpopmail
    user = vpopmail
    password = <password>
  @}
@}
@end example

Authentication flow:

@example
IMAP client (port 993 or 143+STARTTLS)
    |
    | LOGIN user@@domain password (over TLS)
    v
Dovecot auth process
    |
    | SQL passdb: SELECT pw_passwd, pw_dir FROM vpopmail
    |             WHERE pw_name='user' AND pw_domain='domain'
    |
    | verify SHA-512 crypt hash (SHA512-CRYPT scheme)
    |
    +-- success:
    |   uid=89 (vpopmail), gid=89 (vchkpw)
    |   home = pw_dir (e.g. /home/vpopmail/domains/domain/user)
    |   mail = ~/Maildir  (INBOX is the Maildir root)
    |
    +-- failure: IMAP NO Authentication failed
@end example

@section IMAP Authentication — System Users

Unix system users authenticate against
@file{/etc/dovecot/system-passwd} (owned @code{root:dovecot 640}),
which uses standard @code{passwd(5)} format with a @code{@{PLAIN@}}
or @code{@{CRYPT@}} password prefix:

@example
username:@{PLAIN@}clearpassword:uid:gid:gecos:homedir:shell
username:@{CRYPT@}$6$...:uid:gid:gecos:homedir:shell
@end example

The auth chain tries the SQL passdb first; if the user is not found
in the vpopmail table, Dovecot falls through to the @code{passwd-file}
passdb.  @code{auth_username_format = %@{user|username@}} strips the
domain component before the lookup, so @email{fade@@example.com} matches
the @code{fade} entry.

@section TLS Certificate

A single combined PEM file at @file{/etc/qmail/servercert.pem} serves
both qmail-smtpd (STARTTLS on ports 25 and 587) and, indirectly,
Dovecot (whose certificate is extracted from it by the
@command{dovecot-vpopmail} postinst):

@example
/etc/qmail/servercert.pem   root:vchkpw 640   qmail TLS (cert + key)
/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem   root:dovecot 644   Dovecot TLS cert
/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key   root:dovecot 600   Dovecot TLS key
@end example

The @code{vchkpw} group ownership on @file{servercert.pem} is required
because @command{qmail-smtpd} runs as @code{gid=vchkpw} and must read
the private key to perform the TLS handshake.


@c ============================================================
@node Operations
@chapter Operations — TL;DR

@section Adding a Virtual Domain

@example
# 1. Create the domain in vpopmail and PostgreSQL
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vadddomain example.com postmaster-password

# vadddomain also:
#   - adds example.com to /etc/qmail/rcpthosts
#   - adds "example.com:example.com" to /etc/qmail/virtualdomains
#   - adds "+example.com-:..." to /var/lib/qmail/users/assign
#   - runs qmail-newu to rebuild users.cdb
#   - creates /home/vpopmail/domains/example.com/

# 2. Signal qmail-send to re-read virtualdomains
sudo svc -h /etc/service/qmail-send

# 3. Verify
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vdominfo example.com
@end example

@section Adding a Virtual User

@example
# Add user to an existing domain
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vadduser user@@example.com password

# Change a password
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vpasswd user@@example.com

# Delete a user (removes Maildir)
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vdeluser user@@example.com

# List users in a domain
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vdominfo -v example.com

# Show user details (uid, dir, quota)
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vuserinfo user@@example.com

# Set a mailbox quota (100 MB)
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vsetuserquota user@@example.com 102400S
@end example

@section Setting Up a System User for IMAP

System users are Unix accounts (e.g., shell users) who receive mail in
their home directory Maildir and authenticate to Dovecot using a
password in @file{/etc/dovecot/system-passwd}.

@example
# 1. Create the Unix user and Maildir
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash fade
sudo mkdir -p /home/fade/Maildir/@{new,cur,tmp@}
sudo chown -R fade:fade /home/fade/Maildir
echo './Maildir/' | sudo tee /home/fade/.qmail
sudo chown fade:fade /home/fade/.qmail

# 2. Add IMAP credentials to system-passwd
#    Use @{PLAIN@} for cleartext or @{CRYPT@} for a hashed password.
#    To generate a SHA-512 hash: openssl passwd -6 'thepassword'
HASH=$(openssl passwd -6 'thepassword')
printf '%s\n' \
  "fade:@{CRYPT@}$HASH:$(id -u fade):$(id -g fade)::/home/fade:/bin/bash" \
  | sudo tee -a /etc/dovecot/system-passwd
sudo doveadm reload

# 3. Route fade@@example.com to the system user via qmail users/assign
#    The entry must appear BEFORE the +example.com- wildcard.
#    vadddomain inserts wildcards; add the exact match with sed:
sudo sed -i "/^\\.$/i =example.com-fade:fade:$(id -u fade):$(id -g fade):/home/fade:::" \
  /var/lib/qmail/users/assign
sudo /var/lib/qmail/bin/qmail-newu

# 4. Verify
sudo doveadm auth test fade@@example.com thepassword
sudo doveadm user fade@@example.com
@end example

@subsection Routing Mail to a System User

qmail's virtualdomains mechanism rewrites @email{fade@@example.com} to
the local address @code{example.com-fade}.  The exact-match entry in
@file{/var/lib/qmail/users/assign} (prefix @code{=}) takes precedence
over the wildcard entry (prefix @code{+}) that routes all other
@code{example.com} mail to vpopmail:

@example
=example.com-fade:fade:1000:1000:/home/fade:::   ← exact match, wins
+example.com-:example.com:89:89:/home/vpopmail/domains/example.com:-::
.
@end example

@section Removing a Virtual Domain

@cartouche
@strong{Warning:} @command{vdeldomain} permanently removes the domain
directory and all user Maildirs.  This cannot be undone.
@end cartouche

@example
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vdeldomain example.com
sudo svc -h /etc/service/qmail-send
@end example

@section Service Management

@example
# Status of all qmail services
sudo svstat /etc/service/qmail-send
sudo svstat /etc/service/qmail-smtpd
sudo svstat /etc/service/qmail-submission

# Start / stop / restart
sudo svc -u /etc/service/qmail-send       # up
sudo svc -d /etc/service/qmail-send       # down
sudo svc -t /etc/service/qmail-send       # restart (TERM + restart)
sudo svc -h /etc/service/qmail-send       # reload (HUP — re-reads control files)

# Trigger immediate delivery attempt
sudo svc -a /etc/service/qmail-send       # ALRM signal

# Dovecot
sudo systemctl status dovecot
sudo doveadm reload                       # soft reload, no disconnections
@end example

@section Viewing Logs

qmail logs are in tai64n format; @command{tai64nlocal} converts timestamps.

@example
# Follow delivery log in human-readable form
sudo tail -f /var/log/qmail/send/current | tai64nlocal

# Recent SMTP reception log
sudo cat /var/log/qmail/smtpd/current | tai64nlocal | tail -50

# Submission port log
sudo cat /var/log/qmail/submission/current | tai64nlocal | tail -50

# Dovecot auth events (via journald)
sudo journalctl -u dovecot -n 50 --no-pager
@end example

@section Queue Management

@example
# Number of messages currently queued
/var/lib/qmail/bin/qmail-qstat

# Detailed queue listing (sender, recipient, age)
/var/lib/qmail/bin/qmail-qread

# Force immediate retry of deferred messages
sudo svc -a /etc/service/qmail-send
@end example

@section Verifying the Stack

@command{dsmr-verify} runs a comprehensive end-to-end test of all
56 checks.  It requires temporary test fixtures to be pre-created
(see its output for details):

@example
sudo dsmr-verify
@end example

For a quick smoke test without fixtures:

@example
# Packages installed
dpkg -l | grep -E 'dsmr|dovecot-vpopmail|qmail|vpopmail'

# Services up
sudo svstat /etc/service/qmail-send \
            /etc/service/qmail-smtpd \
            /etc/service/qmail-submission
sudo systemctl is-active dovecot

# PostgreSQL connectivity
sudo /home/vpopmail/bin/vdominfo   # lists all registered domains
@end example

@section Relay Control

Edit @file{/etc/qmail/tcp.smtp} then recompile the CDB:

@example
# /etc/qmail/tcp.smtp format:
# 127.:allow,RELAYCLIENT=""
# 1.2.3.4:allow,RELAYCLIENT=""
# :allow

sudo tcprules /etc/qmail/tcp.smtp.cdb /etc/qmail/tcp.smtp.tmp \
  < /etc/qmail/tcp.smtp
@end example

The submission port (587) does not use @file{tcp.submission} for relay
control — @env{SMTPAUTH="!"} enforces authentication instead.

@section DKIM

The postinst generates a 2048-bit RSA key at first install.

@example
# View the DNS TXT record to publish
DOMAIN=$(cat /etc/qmail/defaultdomain)
KEY=$(ls /etc/qmail/domainkeys/$DOMAIN/ 2>/dev/null | head -1)
cat /etc/qmail/domainkeys/$DOMAIN/$KEY.pub
# DNS record name:  $KEY._domainkey.$DOMAIN
# DNS record value: v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=<base64>
@end example

@section Replacing the TLS Certificate

@example
# Install a Let's Encrypt certificate (combined PEM)
sudo cat /etc/letsencrypt/live/hostname/fullchain.pem \
         /etc/letsencrypt/live/hostname/privkey.pem \
  > /etc/qmail/servercert.pem
sudo chmod 640 /etc/qmail/servercert.pem
sudo chown root:vchkpw /etc/qmail/servercert.pem

# Signal qmail-smtpd and qmail-submission to re-read the cert
sudo svc -t /etc/service/qmail-smtpd /etc/service/qmail-submission

# Regenerate Dovecot's copy
sudo rm /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem /etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key
sudo dpkg-reconfigure dovecot-vpopmail
@end example


@c ============================================================
@node qmail
@chapter qmail

qmail is the Mail Transfer Agent at the heart of the DSMR stack.  The
DSMR package tracks the @emph{sagredo-dev} patchset on top of netqmail
1.06, adding the patches listed below without altering qmail's core
security model.

@section Patches Included

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .15 .60
@headitem Patch @tab Version @tab Function
@item TLS @tab 20231230 @tab STARTTLS for SMTP and SMTPS; OpenSSL 3.x
@item DKIM @tab 1.48 @tab RFC 6376 signing (spawn-filter) and verification
@item SPF @tab rc5 @tab Sender Policy Framework checking
@item SRS @tab — @tab Sender Rewriting Scheme (via libsrs2)
@item chkuser @tab 2.0.9 @tab Recipient verification at SMTP time against vpopmail
@item qmail-dnsbl @tab — @tab DNSBL rejection
@item EAI/SMTPUTF8 @tab — @tab RFC 6531 international addresses
@item helodnscheck @tab — @tab Forward-confirmed reverse DNS on EHLO
@end multitable

@section Filesystem Layout

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Path @tab Contents
@item @file{/usr/bin/}, @file{/usr/sbin/} @tab qmail binaries
@item @file{/var/lib/qmail/} @tab Runtime home (queue, control, users, boot)
@item @file{/etc/qmail/} @tab Symlink → @file{/var/lib/qmail/control/}
@item @file{/var/qmail/} @tab Compatibility symlink → @file{/var/lib/qmail/}
@item @file{/var/lib/qmail/queue/} @tab Mail queue
@item @file{/var/log/qmail/} @tab Multilog log directories
@end multitable

@section Key Control Files

All paths relative to @file{/etc/qmail/}:

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem File @tab Purpose
@item @file{me} @tab FQDN of this server
@item @file{defaultdomain} @tab Domain appended to bare addresses
@item @file{locals} @tab Locally-delivered domains
@item @file{rcpthosts} @tab Accepted recipient domains
@item @file{virtualdomains} @tab Virtual domain routing rules
@item @file{servercert.pem} @tab TLS cert + key (combined PEM, @code{root:vchkpw 640})
@item @file{domainkeys/} @tab DKIM private keys per domain
@item @file{filterargs} @tab spawn-filter arguments (enables DKIM)
@item @file{spfbehavior} @tab SPF action (0=off 1=log 2=reject)
@item @file{tcp.smtp.cdb} @tab Compiled relay rules for port 25
@item @file{tcp.submission.cdb} @tab Compiled relay rules for port 587
@item @file{concurrencyincoming} @tab Max concurrent SMTP connections
@item @file{softlimit} @tab Memory limit per smtpd process (bytes)
@end multitable

@xref{dsmr-qmail-run}, for the run scripts that launch these services.


@c ============================================================
@node vpopmail
@chapter vpopmail

vpopmail 5.6.13 manages virtual mail domains and users independently
of Unix system accounts.  The DSMR build uses a PostgreSQL authentication
backend (@option{--enable-auth-module=pgsql}) and disables clear-text
password storage (@option{--enable-clear-passwd=n}).

@section PostgreSQL Database

The postinst auto-creates the database and role on first installation.
Credentials are stored in @file{/home/vpopmail/etc/vpopmail.pgsql}
(owned @code{root:vchkpw 640}):

@example
host|port|user|password|database
localhost|5432|vpopmail|<random-password>|vpopmail
@end example

The primary PostgreSQL tables:

@table @code
@item vpopmail
One row per virtual user.  Key columns: @code{pw_name}, @code{pw_domain},
@code{pw_passwd} (SHA-512 crypt hash), @code{pw_dir} (Maildir parent).

@item dir_control
One row per domain.  Controls the Maildir subdirectory distribution
scheme used for domains with large user counts.
@end table

@section Filesystem Layout

@example
/home/vpopmail/
├── bin/            vpopmail utilities
├── domains/
│   └── example.com/
│       ├── .qmail-default     # "| vdelivermail '' delete"
│       ├── postmaster/
│       │   └── Maildir/
│       └── user/
│           ├── .qmail
│           └── Maildir/
│               ├── cur/
│               ├── new/       ← inbound mail lands here
│               └── tmp/
└── etc/
    └── vpopmail.pgsql         PostgreSQL credentials (root:vchkpw 640)
@end example

@section vpopmail Utilities

@table @command
@item vadddomain @var{domain} @var{postmaster-pass}
Create a virtual domain.  Also updates @file{rcpthosts},
@file{virtualdomains}, and @file{users/assign}.

@item vdeldomain @var{domain}
Delete a virtual domain and all its users and Maildirs.

@item vdominfo [@var{domain}]
Display domain information; with no argument lists all domains.

@item vadduser @var{user@@domain} @var{password}
Add a virtual user.

@item vdeluser @var{user@@domain}
Delete a virtual user.

@item vpasswd @var{user@@domain}
Change a user's password interactively.

@item vuserinfo @var{user@@domain}
Display user details (uid, dir, quota).

@item vsetuserquota @var{user@@domain} @var{quota}
Set mailbox quota (e.g., @code{102400S} for 100 MB).

@item vchkpw
Authentication binary (setuid root, @code{root:vchkpw}).
Invoked by qmail-smtpd on port 587; not called directly by administrators.
@end table


@c ============================================================
@node dovecot-vpopmail
@chapter dovecot-vpopmail

@code{dovecot-vpopmail} is a configuration-only Debian package
(Architecture: all, no compiled code).  It installs conf.d snippets
and a postinst that wires Dovecot 2.4 into the DSMR vpopmail stack.

@section Why SQL Instead of Native vpopmail Drivers

Dovecot 2.4 removed the @code{vpopmail} and @code{checkpassword} passdb
drivers present in 2.3.  The only supported path for authenticating
vpopmail users with Dovecot 2.4 is the @code{sql} passdb driver,
querying the vpopmail table directly.

@section Installed Configuration

@multitable @columnfractions .50 .50
@headitem File @tab Purpose
@item @file{conf.d/05-vpopmail-auth.conf} @tab Auth mechanisms; load SQL passdb before PAM
@item @file{conf.d/vpopmail-auth-sql.conf.ext} @tab passdb/userdb definitions
@item @file{conf.d/99-vpopmail-mail.conf} @tab Maildir layout, uid/gid, INBOX path
@item @file{conf.d/99-vpopmail-master.conf} @tab Auth socket permissions
@item @file{conf.d/99-vpopmail-ssl.conf} @tab TLS required
@end multitable

@noindent
The following files are @emph{generated} by postinst (not conffiles;
safe to delete and regenerate):

@multitable @columnfractions .55 .25 .20
@headitem File @tab Owner/Mode @tab Contents
@item @file{/etc/dovecot/dovecot-vpopmail-sql.conf.ext} @tab root:dovecot 640 @tab PostgreSQL connection block
@item @file{/etc/dovecot/system-passwd} @tab root:dovecot 640 @tab System user passwords
@item @file{/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.pem} @tab root:dovecot 644 @tab TLS certificate
@item @file{/etc/dovecot/private/dovecot.key} @tab root:dovecot 600 @tab TLS private key
@end multitable

@section Regenerating Generated Files

If @file{vpopmail.pgsql} is updated (e.g., after a password rotation):

@example
sudo dpkg-reconfigure dovecot-vpopmail
sudo doveadm reload
@end example

@section Adding a System User

@xref{Operations, Setting Up a System User for IMAP}, for the complete
procedure.  In brief: add an entry to @file{/etc/dovecot/system-passwd}
and an exact-match entry to @file{/var/lib/qmail/users/assign}.


@c ============================================================
@node dsmr-qmail-run
@chapter dsmr-qmail-run

@code{dsmr-qmail-run} installs and manages the three daemontools
supervised service trees for qmail.  It conflicts with the upstream
@code{qmail-run} package.

@section Services

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .10 .60
@headitem Service @tab Port @tab Description
@item @file{qmail-send} @tab — @tab Queue runner + local/remote delivery
@item @file{qmail-smtpd} @tab 25 @tab Inbound SMTP (open relay restricted by CDB)
@item @file{qmail-submission} @tab 587 @tab Authenticated submission (vchkpw required)
@end multitable

Service trees live at @file{/usr/lib/dsmr-qmail-run/supervise/} and are
symlinked into @file{/etc/service/} by the postinst.

@section Run Script Behaviour

All three run scripts derive runtime values from the system at startup:

@table @env
@item QMAILDUID
Set to @code{id -u vpopmail} (uid 89).  @command{qmail-smtpd} and
@command{qmail-send} run as this uid.

@item NOFILESGID
Set to @code{id -g vpopmail} (gid 89 = vchkpw).  The process's
primary group; also the group that must be able to read
@file{servercert.pem}.

@item SOFTLIMIT
Reads @file{/etc/qmail/softlimit} (bytes); enforced via
@command{softlimit}.

@item MAXSMTPD
Reads @file{/etc/qmail/concurrencyincoming}; controls
@command{tcpserver} connection limit.
@end table

@section Fresh Install vs. Upgrade Behaviour

On fresh install the postinst brings each supervised service up with
@code{svc -u} immediately after symlinking.  On upgrade it sends
@code{svc -t} (TERM + restart) to @command{qmail-send} and
@command{qmail-smtpd} to apply updated run scripts without dropping
connections; the submission service is restarted in the same way.

@section DKIM via spawn-filter

If @file{/etc/qmail/filterargs} exists, the @command{qmail-send} run
script sets:

@example
QMAILREMOTE=/var/lib/qmail/bin/spawn-filter
@end example

@command{spawn-filter} reads @file{filterargs} to determine signing
parameters (key path, selector, domain) and calls the DKIM signing
binary before handing the message to @command{qmail-remote}.


@c ============================================================
@node ucspi-ssl
@chapter ucspi-ssl

@code{ucspi-ssl} 0.13.07 (the fehcom.de fork) provides TLS support
for djbware services via the UCSPI interface.  In the DSMR stack it
supplies the TLS primitives used by qmail-smtpd's built-in STARTTLS
implementation (statically linked via fehqlibs).

@section Provided Programs

@table @command
@item sslserver
Accepts TLS connections; analogous to @command{tcpserver}.

@item sslclient
Initiates TLS connections; analogous to @command{tcpclient}.

@item sslio
STARTTLS relay: bridges a plain-text UCSPI service to a TLS channel.
@end table

@section Build Dependencies

ucspi-ssl is compiled against @code{fehqlibs-dev} for DNS resolution
and @code{libssl-dev} (OpenSSL 3.x) for TLS.  It supports TLS 1.3
and is compatible with OpenSSL 3 and LibreSSL.

@section Usage Note

The DSMR submission run script invokes @command{qmail-smtpd} directly
with STARTTLS capability built in (compiled via the TLS patch).
@command{sslserver} is available for services that require a separate
TLS wrapper rather than built-in STARTTLS.


@c ============================================================
@node fehqlibs
@chapter fehqlibs

@code{fehqlibs-dev} 30 is a collection of static libraries by Erwin
Hoffman (@url{https://www.fehcom.de/}) that provide DNS resolution,
network, buffer, and string primitives used by qmail and ucspi-ssl.

@section Contents

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Library @tab Role
@item @file{libqlibs.a} @tab Buffer, string, socket, environment routines
@item @file{libdnsresolv.a} @tab DNSSEC-aware recursive resolver
@end multitable

Headers and libraries install to @file{/usr/lib/fehqlibs/}.  The
package is a build-time dependency only; it installs no runtime
components and leaves no shared libraries in the dynamic linker path.

@section Why a Separate Package

The sagredo-dev qmail patchset and ucspi-ssl both depend on fehqlibs.
Packaging fehqlibs-dev separately ensures both can be built and
upgraded consistently without vendoring the source into each package.


